1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:08,348
Today we’re going to look at another natural food product

3
00:00:08,348 --> 00:00:10,360
and that’s maple syrup.

4
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:12,850
What is this exactly?

5
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:16,597
Well, maple syrup looks rather like clear honey,

6
00:00:16,597 --> 00:00:19,943
but it’s not made by bees;

7
00:00:19,943 --> 00:00:24,711
it’s produced from the plant fluid – or sap – inside the maple tree

8
00:00:24,711 --> 00:00:29,520
and that makes maple syrup a very natural product.

9
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:36,453
Maple syrup is a thick, golden, sweet-tasting liquid that can be bought in bottles

10
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:41,563
or jars and poured onto food such as waffles and ice cream

11
00:00:41,563 --> 00:00:45,050
or used in the baking of cakes and pastries.

12
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:49,164
It contains no preservatives or added ingredients,

13
00:00:49,164 --> 00:00:53,700
and it provides a healthy alternative to refined sugar.

14
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:58,768
Let’s just talk a bit about the maple tree itself,

15
00:00:58,768 --> 00:01:00,960
which is where maple syrup comes from.

16
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:05,152
So, there are many species of maple tree,

17
00:01:05,152 --> 00:01:11,222
and they’ll grow without fertilizer in areas where there’s plenty of moisture in the

18
00:01:11,222 --> 00:01:11,650
soil.

19
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:18,507
However, they’ll only do this if another important criterion is fulfilled,

20
00:01:18,507 --> 00:01:23,880
which is that they must have full or partial sun exposure during the day

21
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:28,130
and very cool nights – and I’ll talk more about that in a minute.

22
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:33,625
There are only certain parts of the world that provide all these conditions:

23
00:01:33,625 --> 00:01:35,469
one is Canada,

24
00:01:35,469 --> 00:01:38,879
and by that, I mean all parts of Canada,

25
00:01:38,879 --> 00:01:43,190
and the other is the north-eastern states of North America.

26
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:49,230
In these areas, the climate suits the trees perfectly.

27
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:55,042
In fact, Canada produces over two-thirds of the world’s maple syrup,

28
00:01:55,042 --> 00:01:59,732
which is why the five-pointed maple leaf is a Canadian symbol

29
00:01:59,732 --> 00:02:03,540
and has featured on the flag since 1964.

30
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:07,770
So how did maple syrup production begin?

31
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:14,712
Well, long before Europeans settled in these parts of the world, the indigenous communities

32
00:02:14,712 --> 00:02:17,080
had started producing maple sugar.

33
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:23,586
They bored holes in the trunks of maple trees and used containers made of

34
00:02:23,586 --> 00:02:27,280
tree bark to collect the liquid sap as it poured out.

35
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:31,969
As they were unable to keep the liquid for any length of time –

36
00:02:31,969 --> 00:02:37,319
they didn’t have storage facilities in those days – they boiled the liquid by

37
00:02:37,319 --> 00:02:42,709
placing pieces of rock that had become scorching hot from the sun into the

38
00:02:42,709 --> 00:02:43,470
sap.

39
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:47,023
They did this until it turned into sugar,

40
00:02:47,023 --> 00:02:51,800
and they were then able to use this to sweeten their food and drinks.

41
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:57,097
Since that time, improvements have been made to the process,

42
00:02:57,097 --> 00:03:04,000
but it has changed very little overall.

43
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,300
So let’s look at the production of maple syrup today.

44
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:15,003
Clearly, the maple forests are a valuable resource in many Canadian

45
00:03:15,003 --> 00:03:16,710
and North American communities.

46
00:03:16,710 --> 00:03:22,004
The trees have to be well looked after and they cannot be used to

47
00:03:22,004 --> 00:03:27,760
make syrup until the trunks reach a diameter of around 25 centimetres.

48
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,990
This can take anything up to 40 years.

49
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:37,779
As I’ve already mentioned, maple trees need the right conditions to grow

50
00:03:37,779 --> 00:03:40,010
and also to produce sap.

51
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:40,990
Why is this?

52
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:47,467
Well, what happens is that during a cold night, the tree absorbs water from

53
00:03:47,467 --> 00:03:48,049
the soil,

54
00:03:48,049 --> 00:03:52,240
and that rises through the tree’s vascular system.

55
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:57,719
But then in the warmer daytime, the change in temperature causes the water to

56
00:03:57,719 --> 00:04:00,770
be pushed back down to the bottom of the tree.

57
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:06,596
This continual movement – up and down – leads to the formation of the

58
00:04:06,596 --> 00:04:09,790
sap needed for maple syrup production.

59
00:04:09,790 --> 00:04:18,157
When the tree is ready, it can be tapped and this involves drilling a small hole into the trunk

60
00:04:18,157 --> 00:04:22,330
and inserting a tube into it that ends in a bucket.

61
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:28,393
The trees can often take several taps, though the workers take care not to

62
00:04:28,393 --> 00:04:32,790
cause any damage to the healthy growth of the tree itself.

63
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:38,729
The sap that comes out of the trees consists of 98 percent water

64
00:04:38,729 --> 00:04:41,960
and 2 percent sugar and other nutrients.

65
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:46,356
It has to be boiled so that much of that water evaporates,

66
00:04:46,356 --> 00:04:52,760
and this process has to take place immediately, using what are called evaporators.

67
00:04:52,760 --> 00:05:01,307
These are basically extremely large pans – the sap is poured into these, a fire is built

68
00:05:01,307 --> 00:05:05,520
and the pans are then heated until the sap boils.

69
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:09,299
As it does this, the water evaporates,

70
00:05:09,299 --> 00:05:11,390
and the syrup begins to form.

71
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:16,843
The evaporation process creates large quantities of steam,

72
00:05:16,843 --> 00:05:19,931
and the sap becomes thicker and denser,

73
00:05:19,931 --> 00:05:22,336
and, at just the right moment,

74
00:05:22,336 --> 00:05:27,248
when the sap is thick enough to be called maple syrup, the worker removes

75
00:05:27,248 --> 00:05:27,990
it from the heat.

76
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:36,837
After this process, something called ‘sugar sand’ has to be filtered out as this builds up during the boiling

77
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:41,070
and gives the syrup a cloudy appearance and a slightly gritty taste.

78
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:45,727
Once this has been done, the syrup is ready to be packaged

79
00:05:45,727 --> 00:05:49,240
so that it can be used for a whole variety of products.

80
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:55,879
It takes 40 litres of sap to produce one litre of maple syrup

81
00:05:55,879 --> 00:05:59,990
so you can get an idea of how much is needed!

82
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:03,814
So that’s the basic process.

83
00:06:03,814 --> 00:06:07,000
